WEBVTT


00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:03.479
Have you ever had your
knitting fall off the machine?

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.790
Get your carriage stuck?

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.120
Or find that a stitch
patterns, just not working?

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:14.190
Even the most experienced machine
has one of those days now.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:19.920
And then in this video we'll cover some
of the common things that can happen and

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.720
we'll talk about how to avoid them.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.760
The links at the top of the video
will take you to other troubleshooting

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.360
tutorials available to
subscribers to Knit it Now.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.480
Whether you're a new knitter or using a
new machine for the first time, one of the

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.000
first things you must know is how to
remove the carriage from your machine in

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.110
the middle of a row.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.660
If for some reason the carriage
jams while knitting, don't force it.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:54.720
Serious damage can be caused by a
knot in the yarn or mis-shaped stitch.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:59.910
Follow your machine manual for the
procedure for removing your carriage or

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.760
opening your carriage to release a jam.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:09.170
You're knitting along just fine, or maybe
you just change colors for a Stripe and

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.180
all the stitches drop off.

00:01:12.645 --> 00:01:16.095
More than likely there's no yarn
in the feeder for some reason.

00:01:16.455 --> 00:01:22.575
Maybe the yarn broke or you reached the
end of the yarn, replace the knitting in

00:01:22.575 --> 00:01:26.115
the hooks of the needles or
ravel it out and try again.

00:01:26.865 --> 00:01:32.265
Now to prevent this from happening, take
extra care to confirm that the yarn is in

00:01:32.265 --> 00:01:38.355
the feeder correctly and watch for knots
and the end of the ball or cone of yarn.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.130
Now, here's a tip.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:45.660
If you're reaching the end of the cone or
ball, be sure that there's at least three

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:51.330
times the width of your knitting left, or
you'll run the risk of dropping some or

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.570
all of the stitches off
the machine unexpectedly.

00:01:55.560 --> 00:02:00.570
If you find that stitches are dropped at
the beginning of a row, check the yarn,

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:04.830
take-up spring, make sure the
yarn is threaded correctly

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.440
and the Springs not caught up somewhere.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.474
With some yarns you will need to take up
the Slack in the yarn at the beginning of

00:02:12.474 --> 00:02:13.050
every row.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.000
Also, be sure you're not taking the
carriage too far from the edge of the

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.910
knitting and causing this extra Slack.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:28.860
If a single stitch drops off somewhere or
it's misformed, check for a bent or broken

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:29.430
needle.

00:02:31.765 --> 00:02:38.065
You might notice that a stitch drops here
and there, or your carriage feels stiff

00:02:38.065 --> 00:02:39.655
and it's difficult to push.

00:02:41.535 --> 00:02:45.435
It's probably because there's
yarn tangled in the sinker wheels.

00:02:45.915 --> 00:02:51.075
Remove your carriage from the needle
bed and check and remove any yarn that's

00:02:51.075 --> 00:02:53.475
tangled on the wheels
or brushes underneath.

00:02:54.255 --> 00:02:59.295
Another reason for the carriage being
difficult to push especially hard in one

00:02:59.295 --> 00:03:01.725
direction is static electricity.

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:08.295
One method for removing static is to use a
dryer softener sheet, wipe down the needle

00:03:08.295 --> 00:03:11.865
bed and carriage, the mast,
and even the cone of yarn.

00:03:13.575 --> 00:03:18.855
If you find that an occasional stitch
tucks or doesn't knit properly, or maybe

00:03:18.855 --> 00:03:24.405
all of the stitches are tucking, your
stitch tension is too low or tight for the

00:03:24.405 --> 00:03:24.825
yarn.

00:03:25.635 --> 00:03:31.125
Or it's feeding too tightly, so adjust
your tension and check that the yarn is

00:03:31.125 --> 00:03:32.234
feeding smoothly.

00:03:33.225 --> 00:03:37.334
You might notice a single
stitch tucking repeatedly.

00:03:37.755 --> 00:03:41.745
This is caused by a bent
needle or a bent needle latch.

00:03:42.195 --> 00:03:43.545
Replace the needle.

00:03:44.685 --> 00:03:49.611
When stitches are tucking or they're not
formed correctly at the beginning of a

00:03:49.611 --> 00:03:54.165
row, you're probably knitting too
fast at the end of the previous row.

00:03:54.704 --> 00:03:56.144
Slow down your knitting.

00:03:56.715 --> 00:04:02.055
When you knit too fast at the end of the
row, you pull those last few stitches

00:04:02.055 --> 00:04:03.315
really tight.

00:04:03.915 --> 00:04:07.785
There's no way they'll knit properly
on the next pass of the carriage.

00:04:08.745 --> 00:04:14.295
Quite often, beginners will find loops
of yarn at the edge of their knitting, or

00:04:14.295 --> 00:04:18.584
maybe the yarn gets tangled up in the
sinker wheels underneath the carriage.

00:04:19.725 --> 00:04:24.255
The reason for this is generally that
the yarn take-up spring isn't operating

00:04:24.255 --> 00:04:24.945
correctly.

00:04:24.945 --> 00:04:31.905
Maybe the yarn isn't threaded correctly or
the carriage is taken too far and too fast

00:04:31.905 --> 00:04:33.405
on the previous row.

00:04:34.125 --> 00:04:35.865
So slow down your knitting.

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:41.430
Check the threading of the tensioner and
check to make sure that the spring is not

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.650
caught up and it's doing its job.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:49.410
You're knitting along and you notice that
the knitting is starting to gather up in

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:50.490
one spot.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.850
The stitches are caught
up on the sinker pins.

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:58.260
Well, this is caused by loose
or irregular yarn tension.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:06.240
If you notice this quickly and maybe just
a single stitch is caught up, you can

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.980
often use your latch tool or crochet hook
and a reach down between the needle bed

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:11.730
and your knitting

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.190
pull up and release the stitch.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:19.500
You can also pull the knitting away from
your machine and pop those threads off the

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:20.400
sinker pins.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.830
The trick to both of these is to do it
without dropping stitches or catching more

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:27.870
strands of yarn on the sinker pins.

00:05:28.605 --> 00:05:33.675
If your yarn is not strong enough for
machine knitting or it's old or weak or

00:05:33.675 --> 00:05:37.935
brittle, it's going to break
constantly causing you gray hair.

00:05:38.325 --> 00:05:44.055
Don't struggle if the yarn is fragile when
knitting, it's not going to hold up in a

00:05:44.055 --> 00:05:44.715
garment.

00:05:45.735 --> 00:05:49.365
If you're having trouble knitting,
here are a few things to check.

00:05:50.235 --> 00:05:52.125
Is the yarn threaded correctly?

00:05:53.025 --> 00:05:56.985
Check your tensioner, the yarn
take-up spring, and your carriage.

00:05:58.615 --> 00:06:01.825
Is the yarn weight
appropriate for your machine?

00:06:03.594 --> 00:06:06.474
Is it secure in the knitting carriage

00:06:08.945 --> 00:06:11.284
check for bent or broken needles?

00:06:13.575 --> 00:06:17.474
Is the tension that you're
using correct for the yarn?

00:06:20.455 --> 00:06:24.745
Is there anything caught under the
carriage in the wheels or the brushes.

00:06:27.135 --> 00:06:30.045
And are you knitting at an even pace?

00:06:31.305 --> 00:06:35.145
The more comfortable you are with your
machine, the greater your success.

